Contents


 
1. Executive Summary
Two distinct legal tracks govern this project, and they should not be confused:
•	Protection (Part A) — securing your ownership of what you have created: the code, writing, designs, music, films, characters, the Law of Twelve, the name and the Ω mark. Most of this protection exists automatically; some of it is strengthened by registration and by contracts.
•	Permission (Part B) — the clearances you must obtain before switching on anything that moves real money or verifies real identity: the token economy, fiat purchase of Ω, real card payments, and KYC/AML. These create real legal exposure in your name and must wait for a licensed attorney.
The platform you have built today is safe because every money- and identity-feature is an honest placeholder. Part A tells you how to lock down ownership now. Part B is written so you can hand it to a lawyer and get answers efficiently.
Part A — Worldwide Copyright & Intellectual-Property Protection
A.1  Copyright is automatic almost everywhere
Under the Berne Convention (which binds 180+ countries, including virtually every market you would target), an original work is protected by copyright automatically the moment it is fixed in a tangible form — saved to disk, written down, recorded. You do not have to register, publish, or mark it to own the copyright.
Practical consequences for you:
•	Your source code, page text, lore, scripts, design system, generated music, and artwork are already your copyright as soon as you create them.
•	Protection is recognised across all Berne countries automatically — that is what “worldwide” copyright means in practice.
•	Typical duration is the life of the author plus 70 years (it varies by country and by whether a work is corporate/anonymous).
A.2  What in SYD OMEGA 91717 is protected, and by which right
Asset	Protected by	Notes
Source code (all HTML/JS, the matrix engine)	Copyright (automatic)	Also keep private algorithms as trade secrets — see A.6.
Written lore, the Charter, the Law of Twelve, articles	Copyright (automatic)	Ideas are not protected; your specific expression of them is.
Visual design, the Ω signet, layouts, posters	Copyright + Trademark	Register the Ω mark and name as trademarks — see A.5.
Films, series, scripts, characters	Copyright (automatic)	Register key works; clear any third-party material used.
Generated ambient music	Copyright (automatic)	Yours because it is generated by your own code, not sampled.
The name “SYD OMEGA 91717” and branding	Trademark	Names are not copyrightable; protect them as trademarks.
The 9×9×9 formulas / progression method	Trade secret (and possibly patent)	Methods/maths are weakly covered by copyright; keep secret.
The domain sydomega.com	Contract (registrar) + Trademark	Trademark strengthens your claim to the matching domain.
A.3  The copyright notice (use it everywhere)
A notice is not required for protection, but it deters infringement, defeats an “innocent infringement” defence, and signals ownership. Place this in your site footer, documents, and code headers:
READY-TO-USE NOTICE
© 2025–2026 Sleiman Youssef Dagher. All rights reserved. “SYD OMEGA 91717”, the Ω signet, and all associated text, code, designs, music, characters, and audiovisual works are the exclusive property of Sleiman Youssef Dagher and are protected by copyright and other intellectual-property laws worldwide. No part may be reproduced, distributed, or used to create derivative works without prior written permission.
You already have a strong ownership line in your footer (“100% OWNED BY SLEIMAN YOUSSEF DAGHER”). Add the © year range and the words “All rights reserved” to make it a formal copyright notice.
A.4  Registration — optional, but powerful where you may enforce
Registration is never required to own copyright, but it strengthens enforcement:
•	United States: registration with the U.S. Copyright Office is required before you can sue for infringement, and timely registration unlocks statutory damages and attorney’s fees. If the U.S. is a target market, register your key works (the platform, the films, the core writings).
•	Many other countries offer voluntary registration or deposit systems that provide useful date-stamped evidence of authorship.
•	Low-cost evidence anywhere: keep dated records — your Git history, timestamped exports, and even a sealed copy emailed to yourself — to prove what you created and when.
A.5  Trademark — protect the name and the Ω mark
Copyright does not protect names, short phrases, or logos as brand identifiers — trademark does. Trademarks are territorial; you register them country by country, or use the Madrid Protocol (administered by WIPO) to seek protection in many countries through one application.
•	Consider registering: the word mark “SYD OMEGA 91717” (and “SYD OMEGA”), and the Ω signet logo, in the classes that matter (software/SaaS, entertainment, financial services if the economy launches).
•	Search first: before filing, have an attorney clear the marks against existing registrations to avoid conflicts.
•	Use the symbols: ™ can be used immediately to assert a claim; ® may be used only after registration is granted.
A.6  Trade secrets — your formulas and algorithms
The most valuable and least copyrightable parts — the exact progression maths, weighting, and any proprietary logic — are best protected as trade secrets. A trade secret is protected for as long as it stays secret and you take reasonable steps to keep it so:
•	Keep private logic on the server / in private repositories, not in public client-side code where practical.
•	Bind everyone who sees it (developers, collaborators, contractors) with a written Non-Disclosure Agreement before disclosure.
•	Mark sensitive documents “Confidential — Trade Secret.”
A.7  Contracts — the protection people forget
Your strongest, cheapest protection is paperwork. Make sure of the following:
•	IP assignment from every contributor: anyone who writes code, draws art, composes, or writes for the project must sign that all work is assigned to you (or your company). Without this, a freelancer can own what they made. This is critical — do it before they start work.
•	Your platform Terms (already built) should state that all platform IP is yours and that users get only a limited licence to use the service, and that user-submitted content grants you a licence to display it.
•	NDAs with anyone who sees non-public material.
•	Consider forming a company (e.g., an LLC or local equivalent) to hold the IP — it separates personal liability from the business and makes ownership cleaner. Ask the attorney about this.
A.8  Protection checklist (do these now — no lawyer required for most)
1.	Add the full © notice (A.3) to the site footer, documents, and code headers.
2.	Preserve dated proof of authorship: keep your Git history; export and timestamp key works.
3.	Get a signed IP-assignment + NDA from anyone who has helped or will help build anything.
4.	Keep proprietary formulas out of public code where feasible; mark them Confidential.
5.	Make a shortlist of works/marks to register (key writings, films, the name, the Ω logo) and take it to an attorney.
6.	Secure brand handles and the trademark-matching domains you care about.
 
Part B — The Economic-Layer Brief (hand this to a lawyer)
Everything below is gated in your platform today and must stay gated until a licensed attorney clears it. Give this section to counsel; it states what you intend and the exact questions to resolve.
B.1  What Ω is intended to be
•	Ω is intended as an internal platform asset / utility points system, earned through verified activity, used inside the platform. It is not intended as an investment, currency, or security, and (today) is not purchasable for fiat, not withdrawable, and not transferable off-platform.
•	The first legal question is whether, as actually designed and marketed, Ω would nonetheless be treated as a regulated instrument.
B.2  Securities classification
In the United States, the “Howey test” asks whether people invest money in a common enterprise expecting profit from others’ efforts; many tokens have been deemed securities under it. The European Union now regulates crypto-assets under MiCA, and other countries have their own regimes. Classification depends on the fine details of design and marketing — especially any promise or expectation of profit.
Ask counsel:
•	As designed and marketed, is Ω a security / regulated crypto-asset in each target jurisdiction?
•	What design and marketing rules keep it a pure utility token (e.g., no profit language, no secondary trading, no sale to the public)?
B.3  Money transmission & e-money
If users can buy, cash out, or transfer value, you may be a money transmitter or e-money issuer.
•	United States: this can require federal FinCEN money-services-business registration and state-by-state money-transmitter licences.
•	EU/UK: e-money and payment-services rules (PSD2 and related) may apply.
•	Ask: does our fiat-to-Ω flow or any withdrawal trigger licensing, and how do we avoid or obtain it?
B.4  KYC / AML (identity verification)
Your planned ID/passport/licence check and one-account rule are AML/KYC controls. Financial features typically require a documented AML programme and a vetted KYC provider.
•	Ask: what KYC/AML obligations attach to our features, which licensed provider should we use, and what records must we keep?
B.5  Payments & subscriptions (Stripe)
•	Real card payments and subscriptions require business verification with the payment processor, accurate pricing/terms, refund and cancellation policies, and tax handling (e.g., VAT/sales tax).
•	Ask: what entity, terms, and disclosures do we need before charging real money?
B.6  Data privacy
You already collect emails, and plan to collect phone, country, ID documents, and uploaded files — all personal data.
•	EU/UK GDPR, California CCPA/CPRA, and laws like Canada’s PIPEDA may apply depending on where users are. You generally need a privacy policy, a lawful basis for processing, consent where required, data-subject rights (access/export/delete), breach procedures, and care with sensitive data (ID documents).
•	Ask: which privacy laws apply to us, and what policy + controls do we need? (Note: ID documents and any health/heredity data are especially sensitive.)
B.7  Content licensing & rights clearance
•	You own what you create, but anything third-party — fonts, stock assets, music samples, code libraries, any artwork you did not make — must be properly licensed.
•	Greek myth itself is public domain, but specific modern depictions, images, or text by others are not. Clear everything that is not original to you.
B.8  Astrology / horoscope content
•	Keep clear “for entertainment / reflection only” disclaimers (you already do). The AI agents must not give medical, legal, or financial advice; state this in your terms.
B.9  Features that stay OFF until counsel signs off
Feature	Why gated
Public Marketplace / trading of Ω or NFTs	Securities + money-transmission risk
Buying Ω with real money; withdrawals/transfers	Money transmission / e-money licensing
Real card payments & paid subscriptions	Processor verification, consumer & tax law
ID / passport / licence verification (KYC)	AML/KYC obligations + sensitive-data law
Any “reserve” / asset-backing claims	Securities & consumer-protection / advertising law
 
